    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3cf19cc1-07eb-5993-9bf4-76492a205588",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{(-2)x+(-2)y}{4}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,3)$,$(-1,-1)$,$(-3,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)+(-2)(3)}{4}=- \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-1,-1)}=\\frac{(-2)(-1)+(-2)(-1)}{4}=1, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-3,-1)}=\\frac{(-2)(-3)+(-2)(-1)}{4}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/f5/f51fb78a12bc37626954936b6f001ff807b97c0c55279314ec60f61ecbfde501.png)",
